@group(:concurrency)
mod Std.Otp
  ## The typed BEAM process algebra — the sanctioned concurrency surface.
  ##
  ## Two typed handles, both opaque phantoms erased to a raw `erlang:pid()` at
  ## runtime; their whole job is static — using a process at the WRONG message or
  ## reply type is a COMPILE error, the guarantee `Std.Otp.Raw`'s untyped `Pid`
  ## cannot give (design 2026-07-10-checked-beam-concurrency §2,
  ## 2026-07-09-typed-beam-process-algebra):
  ##
  ##   * `Pid(m)`            — a plain process accepting messages of type `m`
  ##                          (raw `erlang:send`, one-way).
  ##   * `GenServer(q, r)`   — a `gen_server` taking requests `q`; a synchronous
  ##                          `call` returns a reply `r`. The reply type rides on
  ##                          the SERVER'S type, so `call` needs no return-only
  ##                          implicit (which does not resolve through an effectful
  ##                          extern) — a wrong request AND a wrong reply are both
  ##                          caught.
  ##
  ## Every operation performs a BEAM side effect, so it returns `Effect(T)`: the
  ## effect discipline forbids duplicating, dropping, or reordering a
  ## `tell`/`call`/`cast`. Messages are POLYMORPHIC (the narrow point for a message
  ## set), never `Any`.
  ##
  ## v1 declares the typed operations as effect-typed externs over the same stock
  ## BEAM BIFs `Std.Otp.Raw` wraps; the sealing is that `Std.Otp.Raw` is `unsafe`
  ## and `Std.Otp` is the sanctioned surface. Still deferred to the session-typed
  ## ceiling (needs message CODES + the macro facility): `q`/`r` as message-set
  ## codes with `subset`/`reply_of` and the dependent `ReplyOf(req)` reply lookup,
  ## protocol-state evolution, and `spawn`/`start_link` MINTING a typed handle
  ## (a bespoke proc-fn lowering, not a plain extern). This floor is what they
  ## build on; a handle is obtained today by being passed one.

  ## OTP container syntax is a standard-library macro surface. The raw body is
  ## parsed again by the normal Cure parser and lowered by the generic OTP
  ## backend; the compiler has no actor/fsm/supervisor/application classes.
  macro OtpContainers
    syntax actor <name: Identifier> (with <payload: Code>)? <body: raw until dedent> becomes __otp_container(:actor, name, payload, body)
      example actor Cure.Example expands actor Cure.Example
    syntax fsm <name: Identifier> (with <payload: Code>)? <body: raw until dedent> becomes __otp_container(:fsm, name, payload, body)
      example fsm Cure.Example expands fsm Cure.Example
    syntax sup <name: Identifier> (with <payload: Code>)? <body: raw until dedent> becomes __otp_container(:sup, name, payload, body)
      example sup Cure.Example expands sup Cure.Example
    syntax app <name: Identifier> (with <payload: Code>)? <body: raw until dedent> becomes __otp_container(:app, name, payload, body)
      example app Cure.Example expands app Cure.Example
    explain
      Identifier => "a container name is required"
      Code => "a container payload must be a Cure expression"
      keyword "with" => "payloads are introduced with the with keyword"
      keyword "actor" => "starts an OTP actor container"
      keyword "fsm" => "starts an OTP state-machine container"
      keyword "sup" => "starts an OTP supervisor container"
      keyword "app" => "starts an OTP application container"

  ## A typed process handle accepting messages of type `m`.
  opaque type Pid(m)

  ## A typed `gen_server` taking requests `q`, replying `r`.
  opaque type GenServer(q, r)

  ## The calling process's own typed pid; `m` inferred from how the result is used.
  @extern(:erlang, :self, 0)
  fn self() -> Effect(Pid(m))

  ## Send a well-typed message: `msg : m` must match `dest`'s accepted type `m`,
  ## so a `Pid(Command)` can never be sent a `Telemetry`. (Named `tell`, not
  ## `send`: `send` is the reserved Melquiades keyword — a later macro can reclaim
  ## the `p ! msg` surface and lower it to this.)
  @extern(:erlang, :send, 2)
  fn tell(dest: Pid(m), msg: m) -> Effect(Unit)

  ## Synchronous `gen_server` call: send request `q`, block for reply `r`. Both the
  ## request AND the reply are checked against the server's type.
  @extern(:gen_server, :call, 2)
  fn call(server: GenServer(q, r), request: q) -> Effect(r)

  ## Asynchronous `gen_server` cast — a typed fire-and-forget request (no reply).
  @extern(:gen_server, :cast, 2)
  fn cast(server: GenServer(q, r), request: q) -> Effect(Unit)
end
